      <!-- Seven Primitives Top-Down Breakdown -->
      <div class="anatomy-breakdown-intro">
        <h4 class="anatomy-subheading">Deconstructing the Seven Universal Subsystems</h4>
        <p class="ladder-narrative" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          Below is a detailed microarchitectural deconstruction of each subsystem in Figure 1.0, its silicon implementation costs, and how open-source cores diverge across the performance-area spectrum:
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="primitives-flow">
        <!-- Primitive 1 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 01</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Sv39 / Sv48 Radix-Tree Hardware Page Table Walker (PTW)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Translates 39-bit (Sv39) or 48-bit (Sv48) virtual addresses into 56-bit physical memory addresses. When a TLB miss occurs, the hardware PTW autonomously issues sequential DRAM memory reads across a 3-level (or 4-level) radix tree starting from the root base address stored in the privileged <code>satp.PPN</code> register.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Hardware Implementation:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Autonomous FSM with dedicated bus master port, address arithmetic synthesis logic, and PTE validation checker.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Rocket and CVA6 use a shared PTW servicing both ITLB and DTLB; BOOM utilizes dual parallel PTW channels with intermediate level-cache translation buffers.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 2 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 02</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Split Translation Lookaside Buffers (ITLB &amp; DTLB)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              High-speed Content-Addressable Memory (CAM) arrays caching recent Virtual Page Number (VPN) to Physical Page Number (PPN) translations alongside Address Space Identifiers (ASID) and permission bits (Read, Write, Execute, User).
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Hardware Implementation:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Fully associative 32-to-64 entry CAM cells integrated directly into the critical fetch and load-store pipeline stages.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">In-order cores (Rocket, CVA6) implement 32-entry single-level TLBs (~3.5 kGE); out-of-order BOOM uses a 2-level hierarchy (8-entry L1 TLB + 512-entry shared L2 TLB).</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 3 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 03</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Split L1 Caches &amp; Non-Blocking MSHR Subsystem</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Dedicated Harvard L1 Instruction Cache (32 KB, 4-way) and Data Cache (32 KB, 8-way). The Data Cache incorporates <strong>Miss Status Holding Registers (MSHRs)</strong> that track pending cache-line refills from DRAM, allowing subsequent instructions to access cached data without stalling the core (Hit-Under-Miss and Miss-Under-Miss).
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Hardware Implementation:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Multi-bank SRAM macro arrays, write-back dirty bit arrays, comparator tag arrays, and 4-to-8 MSHR entry comparators.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">CVA6 features a write-through D-cache with 2 MSHRs; Rocket provides non-blocking TileLink D-cache with 4 MSHRs; BOOM features 8 MSHRs supporting heavy memory concurrency.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 4 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 04</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Multi-Level Branch Prediction Subsystem (BTB, BHT, RAS &amp; TAGE)</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Because 6-to-10 stage application pipelines incur severe 3 to 7 clock cycle penalties on branch mispredictions, application cores integrate multi-level predictors: Branch Target Buffers (BTB) for fast zero-bubble target caching, Branch History Tables (BHT) for direction, Return Address Stacks (RAS) for function returns, and advanced TAGE algorithms.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Hardware Implementation:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">SRAM tag arrays, 2-bit saturating counters, 16-entry circular RAS stacks, and speculative checkpoint recovery registers.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Rocket uses a 28-entry BTB + 512-entry Bimodal BHT; CVA6 uses a 32-entry BTB + 1024-entry BHT + RAS; BOOM incorporates an industrial multi-kilobyte TAGE-SC predictor achieving &gt;96% accuracy.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 5 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 05</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Privileged Architecture, PMP Firewalls &amp; CSR Trap Unit</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Enforces the RISC-V Privileged Architecture v1.11: Machine (M) mode for firmware (OpenSBI), Supervisor (S) mode for the Linux kernel, and User (U) mode for application binaries. Features Physical Memory Protection (PMP) units checking physical memory accesses against up to 16 programmable address boundaries.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Hardware Implementation:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Privileged CSR bank (<code>mstatus</code>, <code>sstatus</code>, <code>satp</code>, <code>mtvec</code>, <code>stvec</code>), address comparator arrays, and hardware trap redirect FSM.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Rocket and CVA6 implement 8 to 16 PMP regions with single-cycle trap redirection; BlackParrot integrates PMP directly alongside its BedRock channel security isolation.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 6 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 06</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Decoupled Execution: In-Order Scoreboard vs. Out-of-Order RoB</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Coordinates instruction execution across multiple functional units (Integer ALUs, 64-bit Multipliers, FPUs, Load-Store Units). In-order cores utilize scoreboards to track RAW dependencies; out-of-order processors employ Register Alias Tables (RAT), Physical Register Files (PRF), Reservation Stations, and a Reorder Buffer (RoB) to retire instructions in program order while executing out of order.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Hardware Implementation:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Scoreboard bit vectors (in-order) vs. 64-to-128 entry circular FIFO Reorder Buffers with speculative commit pointers (out-of-order).</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Rocket uses an in-order pipeline with bypass multiplexers; CVA6 implements an 8-entry issue scoreboard; BOOM features up to a 128-entry ROB and explicit PRF renaming.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Primitive 7 -->
        <div class="primitive-card">
          <div class="prim-card-header">
            <span class="prim-step-pill">BLOCK 07</span>
            <h4 class="prim-title">Coherent System Bus &amp; Cache Coherence Engine</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="prim-card-body">
            <p class="prim-text">
              Provides standardized high-bandwidth communication with system memory, peripherals, and peer cores. Implements hardware cache coherence protocols (such as directory-based MESI or snooping broadcast) over open interconnect standards like AXI4-64b, TileLink-C, or BedRock.
            </p>
            <div class="prim-micro-insights">
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Hardware Implementation:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">Multi-channel crossbar decoders, transaction queues, coherence state directories, and invalidation probe filters.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="prim-insight-row">
                <span class="insight-label">Design Divergence:</span>
                <span class="insight-value">CVA6 uses standardized 64-bit AXI4 point-to-point buses; Rocket utilizes TileLink-UH/TileLink-C; BlackParrot uses the BedRock channel protocol formally verified with CMurphi.</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.2 THE PRIVILEGED ISA SPECTRUM -->
    <div class="concept-step-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-ink">1.2 THE PRIVILEGED ISA SPECTRUM</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">RV64GC Standard Extensions &amp; Privileged Ring Protection</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        While microcontroller cores often strip down to RV32I or RV32E to minimize silicon gate count, full operating systems like Linux require <strong>RV64GC</strong> (<code>RV64IMAFDC_Zicsr_Zifencei</code>). Below is why each architectural extension is non-negotiable for operating system execution and its physical silicon cost:
      </p>

      <div class="tradeoff-matrix-wrap">
        <table class="tradeoff-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>ISA Extension</th>
              <th>Architectural Purpose</th>
              <th>Silicon Area Overhead</th>
              <th>OS / Linux Role</th>
              <th>Hardware Engineering Justification</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Base RV64I</strong></td>
              <td>64-bit GPRs, 64-bit pointer arithmetic, 64-bit ALUs</td>
              <td>Baseline (~45 kGE core logic)</td>
              <td>Mandatory</td>
              <td>Enables flat &gt;4 GB virtual address spaces, eliminating 32-bit physical memory limits.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>M (Mul / Div)</strong></td>
              <td>Hardware 64-bit integer multiplication and division</td>
              <td>+8 to 15 kGE (Pipelined Booth array)</td>
              <td>Mandatory</td>
              <td>Eliminates 50-to-100 cycle software emulation loops in kernel hash tables and scheduler maths.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>A (Atomics)</strong></td>
              <td>Load-Reserved / Store-Conditional (<code>lr.d</code>/<code>sc.d</code>) and AMOs</td>
              <td>+3 to 6 kGE (Bus reservation monitor)</td>
              <td>Mandatory</td>
              <td>Enables lock-free multi-core synchronization, Linux spinlocks, mutexes, and thread scheduling.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>F &amp; D (FPUs)</strong></td>
              <td>IEEE 754-2008 single and double-precision floating-point</td>
              <td>+35 to 55 kGE (Fused multiply-add units)</td>
              <td>Mandatory for glibc</td>
              <td>Provides 32 dedicated 64-bit FPRs (<code>f0</code>-<code>f31</code>) avoiding soft-float software ABI overhead.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>C (Compressed)</strong></td>
              <td>16-bit instruction encodings for common operations</td>
              <td>+1.5 to 3.0 kGE (Frontend expander)</td>
              <td>Strongly Recommended</td>
              <td>Shrinks Linux kernel binary footprints by 25–30% and increases effective L1-I cache throughput.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Privilege &amp; satp</strong></td>
              <td>Machine, Supervisor, User modes + MMU control</td>
              <td>+12 to 20 kGE (CSRs &amp; trap logic)</td>
              <td>Mandatory</td>
              <td>Hardware isolation preventing unprivileged user tasks from corrupting OS kernel memory.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>PMP (16-Regions)</strong></td>
              <td>Physical Memory Protection address range checking</td>
              <td>+4 to 8 kGE (16 parallel comparators)</td>
              <td>Mandatory for Security</td>
              <td>Hardware memory firewall protecting OpenSBI secure enclaves from rogue supervisor code.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.3 WHY APPLICATION CORES DIVERGE -->
    <div class="concept-step-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-ink">1.3 THE MEMORY WALL TRIAD</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">Why Application Cores Diverge: The Hardware Triad</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        If all application cores implement the exact same RV64GC ISA and run the same compiled Linux distributions, why do their physical gate counts range from <strong>135 kGE up to 420 kGE</strong>?
        The answer lies in how different engineering teams balance the eternal <strong>Application Hardware Triad</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="triad-cards-grid">
        <div class="triad-card">
          <div class="triad-card-head">
            <span class="triad-pill badge-yellow">AXIS 1</span>
            <h4>Silicon Area &amp; Energy Budget</h4>
          </div>
          <p>
            When processors are deployed in edge gateways or battery-powered IoT clusters, die area must remain sub-150 kGE (&lt;0.2 mm&sup2; in 22nm). These designs adopt lean 5-stage in-order pipelines (e.g. Rocket Chip), accepting memory stall bubbles to achieve 15+ mW ultra-low power consumption.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="triad-card">
          <div class="triad-card-head">
            <span class="triad-pill badge-cyan">AXIS 2</span>
            <h4>Memory Hierarchy &amp; Cache Coherence</h4>
          </div>
          <p>
            In multi-core clusters, memory throughput dominates. Teams invest silicon in multi-banked non-blocking L1 caches, 4 to 8 MSHRs, and directory-based MESI coherence controllers (e.g. BlackParrot). This increases area to 165 kGE but prevents bus crossbar saturation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="triad-card">
          <div class="triad-card-head">
            <span class="triad-pill badge-coral">AXIS 3</span>
            <h4>Instruction-Level Parallelism (ILP)</h4>
          </div>
          <p>
            When raw single-thread throughput is paramount (desktop, server, edge AI), in-order processors hit an insurmountable CPI ceiling (~1.25). Out-of-order engines (e.g. SonicBOOM) triple silicon area to 420 kGE to implement 128-entry Reorder Buffers, register renaming, and multi-issue dispatch, driving CPI below 0.85.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.4 WHY FOUR ARCHETYPES -->
    <div class="concept-step-card">
      <div class="step-card-header">
        <span class="step-tag badge-ink">1.4 ARCHITECTURAL PHASE TRANSITIONS</span>
        <h3 class="step-card-title">Why Four Archetypes? The Microarchitectural Boundaries</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="step-card-lead">
        Based on these physical silicon trade-offs, the open-source 64-bit application core ecosystem clusters into four distinct microarchitectural archetypes:
      </p>

      <div class="archetype-preview-deck">
        <div class="arch-preview-item" style="border-left: 6px solid var(--yellow);">
          <span class="api-num">ARCHETYPE 1</span>
          <h4>Classic In-Order Baseline (Rocket Chip &bull; ~135 kGE)</h4>
          <p>Single-issue 5-stage classic RISC pipeline with TileLink-UH memory hierarchy and integrated Sv39 MMU. The reference baseline for academic research and low-power Linux.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="arch-preview-item" style="border-left: 6px solid var(--cyan);">
          <span class="api-num">ARCHETYPE 2</span>
          <h4>Industrial Deep In-Order Core (CVA6 / Ariane &bull; ~148 kGE)</h4>
          <p>6-stage decoupled scoreboard pipeline with standardized AXI4-64b system bus interface, 16 PMP regions, and branch prediction. Hardened for industrial SoC tapeouts.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="arch-preview-item" style="border-left: 6px solid var(--coral);">
          <span class="api-num">ARCHETYPE 3</span>
          <h4>Modular Coherent Multi-Core Cluster Tile (BlackParrot &bull; ~165 kGE)</h4>
          <p>5-stage frontend with dual-issue dispatch, decoupled backend execution, and formally verified directory-based MESI cache coherence over the BedRock interconnect.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="arch-preview-item" style="border-left: 6px solid var(--lime);">
          <span class="api-num">ARCHETYPE 4</span>
          <h4>High-Performance Superscalar OoO Engine (SonicBOOM &bull; ~420 kGE)</h4>
          <p>7-to-10 stage out-of-order execution engine with 1-to-4 wide decode/dispatch, explicit register renaming, 128-entry Reorder Buffer (ROB), and non-blocking L1D caches.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Benchmark Leaderboard -->
